          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which alarm features in reflux does guidance say should change the plan?",
          "answer": "Guidance on reflux (GERD) lists a set of \"alarm features.\" When one is present, the usual approach changes: the guideline advises prompt endoscopy (a camera test of the upper gut) rather than starting an empiric trial of acid-suppressing medicine first. Source: \"GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it\" (gastroenterology society guideline summary).\n\nThe alarm features it lists are:\n- Difficulty swallowing, or pain on swallowing.\n- Unintentional weight loss.\n- Gastrointestinal bleeding, such as vomiting blood or black tarry stools.\n- Iron-deficiency anaemia.\n- Persistent vomiting.\n- A palpable mass, or a family history of upper gastrointestinal cancer.\n- New symptoms starting at an older age.\n\nSome symptoms need prompt medical attention now. The general reflux summary says chest discomfort should not be assumed to be reflux. Chest discomfort brought on by exertion, or with breathlessness, sweating, nausea, or pain spreading to the arm, neck or jaw, needs emergency assessment for a heart cause first. If you have these symptoms, call your local emergency number now. Vomiting blood, black tarry stools, difficulty swallowing and unintentional weight loss also need prompt medical assessment (source: \"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD),\" general clinical reference).\n\nThe retrieved material does not explain how endoscopy results are interpreted, or how to weigh each alarm feature in a particular person. It also does not cover the lifestyle and medicine steps for people without alarm features beyond a brief outline. A clinician is the right person to decide what applies to you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
